.TH TURBOJEDI 1 "October 2026" "TurboJedi" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
turbojedi \- Turbo Vision viewer of the systemd journal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B turbojedi
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIFIELD\fB=\fIVALUE\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B turbojedi
shows the systemd journal in windows and follows it.
Every window is a journal client of its own: its filter goes to the journal as a query.
Options set the filter of the first window, the dialogs of the Journal menu change the filter of the focused one.
.PP
\fIFIELD\fB=\fIVALUE\fR matches any journal field.
Matches of the same field are joined by OR, of different fields by AND.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-identifier\fR \fIID\fR
Entries of SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER \fIID\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-unit\fR \fIUNIT\fR
Entries of systemd unit \fIUNIT\fR, \fB.service\fR is assumed without a suffix. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-U\fR, \fB\-\-user\fR \fIUSER\fR
Entries of user \fIUSER\fR, by name or number. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-priority\fR \fILEVEL\fR
Entries up to \fILEVEL\fR: emerg, alert, crit, err, warning, notice, info, debug or 0..7.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-kernel\fR
Kernel messages only.
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-since\fR \fITIME\fR
Entries from \fITIME\fR on instead of the last ones.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fITIME\fR
Entries up to \fITIME\fR, the window stops following then.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-boot\fR
Entries of the current boot only.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-directory\fR \fIDIR\fR
Journal files of \fIDIR\fR instead of the local journal, in the first window.
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-merge\fR
Journals of every machine there are, like \fBjournalctl \-\-merge\fR: other machine IDs in
\fI/var/log/journal\fR and \fI/var/log/journal/remote\fR of
.BR systemd-journal-remote (8),
instead of this machine only.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-lines\fR \fIN\fR
History to start with, 1000 by default.
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\fR
The newest entries on top in the first window, like \fBjournalctl \-\-reverse\fR.
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-highlight\fR \fINAME\fR
Rules that paint messages: \fBbuiltin\fR (default), \fBoff\fR, a rule file of
.BR grc (1)
such as \fBconf.log\fR from \fI~/.grc\fR, \fI/usr/local/share/grc\fR or \fI/usr/share/grc\fR, or a path to one.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-layout\fR \fIFILE\fR
Windows of a layout saved by File, Save layout. Options are left for new windows then.
.TP
\fB\-\-clipboard\-copy\fR \fICOMMAND\fR
Command of
.BR sh (1)
that takes copied lines on its standard input instead of the clipboard of the terminal, such as
\fBremote-copy.sh\fR of RemoteClipboard. It goes over \fBTURBOJEDI_CLIPBOARD_COPY\fR. When the command exits
with an error or does not finish in 3 seconds, the clipboard of the terminal takes the lines.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Usage.
.PP
\fITIME\fR is \fB2026-10-23 19:00\fR[\fB:00\fR[\fB.000000\fR]], \fB2026-10-23\fR, \fB19:00\fR (today), \fBnow\fR, \fBtoday\fR,
\fByesterday\fR, \fBtomorrow\fR or relative as journalctl takes it: \fB\-1h\fR, \fB+30m\fR, \fB"\-1h 30min"\fR,
\fB"5min ago"\fR (us, ms, s, min, h, d, w, M, y and their longer names); in local time, or in UTC with \fBUTC\fR at the
end as journalctl takes it: \fB"2026-10-23 17:00 UTC"\fR.
.SH KEYS
.TP 18
.B F1
Keys.
.TP
.B F2
New window with the filter of the command line.
.TP
.B Shift-F2
Clone of the focused window: its lines, filter, find, selection and place, or the counts, step, scale, bars and cursor of a timeline; both go on with the journal on their own.
.TP
.B F3
Levels.
.TP
.B F4
Text filter: plain or regular expression, to show or to leave out the matching entries, case sensitive or not.
.TP
.B F7
Source: unit, identifier, command, transport, boot, user, machine, PID, with lists of the values in the journal.
.TP
.B Shift-F7
Journals of the window: of this machine, of every machine there is, like \fBjournalctl \-\-merge\fR, or of a directory.
.TP
.B Ctrl-T
Time range, in local time or with \fBUTC\fR checked in UTC: checking it turns the times over to the same moments,
\fBtoday\fR to the date and time of its midnight; it starts in the zone the range is written in, or as the window
shows times when nothing in the range is of a zone.
.TP
.B Ctrl-K
Columns: date, time, level, host, unit, identifier, PID; UTC, milliseconds, wrapped messages, hidden heading,
the newest on top.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Wrap long messages.
.TP
.B Ctrl-R
The newest entries on top or at the bottom, the lines of a message stay under it. The title of the window ends
with \[u25B2] when the newest are at the bottom and with \[u25BC] when they are on top, a click on it turns the order.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Highlighting rules of the window.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F
Find and highlight. \fBCtrl-N\fR and \fBCtrl-P\fR go to the next and previous one,
the search goes on into the older entries past the oldest line.
.TP
.B Ctrl-G
Go to a time, given the way \fB\-\-since\fR takes it; the one of the line at hand in the zone the window shows.
.TP
.B Alt-G
Every other window goes to the time of the selected line, their filters stay as they are.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Z
Undo navigation: back along the moves of the window, also \fBAlt-Left\fR and \fBAlt-Backspace\fR. Every move of the
cursor by the keys or the mouse, \fBCtrl-G\fR, \fBAlt-G\fR and a find is one, the last 64 of them; marks and lines are
not undone.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Y
Redo navigation: forward again where \fBCtrl-Z\fR has come from, also \fBAlt-Right\fR. A new move after \fBCtrl-Z\fR
drops the way ahead.
.TP
.B Alt-T
Timeline: entries of the filter of the window by intervals of time, errors, warnings and the rest stacked
in a column per interval. \fBLeft\fR and \fBRight\fR move the cursor, \fBEnter\fR takes every journal window
to the first entry of the interval, \fB+\fR and \fB\-\fR make the intervals finer or coarser,
\fBL\fR switches between linear and logarithmic scale, \fBB\fR between bars of blocks and of Braille dots,
two intervals a cell, \fBU\fR between hours and days of UTC and of the local zone, as the window shows times at
first. \fBShift\fR with the arrows, dragging or \fBShift\fR+click select intervals, \fBZ\fR or a
double click in the selection zooms into it, \fBCtrl\-T\fR sets the range, \fBCtrl\-Z\fR or a right click and
\fBCtrl\-Y\fR go back and forward along the ranges and the steps with the cursor. Intervals of hours above one and of
days go by the wall clock, a day the clocks change has 23 or 25 hours.
.TP
.B Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End
Move the cursor, which starts on the line of the window nearest to the newest entries. On the newest line the
window follows the tail, anywhere else it pauses.
.TP
.B Enter
All fields of the entry under the cursor, also by a double click.
\fBEnter\fR on a field there opens a window with the filter of this one and the field.
.TP
.B Alt-F10, A
Actions of the plugins for the entry under the cursor, also \fBF11\fR, \fBShift-F10\fR and a right click on a line;
\fBA\fR is for Terminal of macOS, which keeps F10 and F11 for itself.
See \fBPLUGINS\fR below.
.TP
.B Space
Pause or follow the tail. Scrolling past the oldest line brings the older entries in.
The title of a window starts with \[u25BA] when it follows, with \[u2016] when it is paused, and with the count of the
entries come meanwhile after it; a click on the sign pauses or follows. A paused window that is full keeps its lines
and marks, the new entries are counted and come in when it follows again.
.TP
.B Shift+arrows
Select lines from the cursor, also with the mouse. \fBEsc\fR drops the selection and the marks, the cursor stays.
.TP
.B M, Insert
Begin marking from the cursor, the keys mark the lines they pass. \fBM\fR again ends it and they stay marked,
so lines apart are marked one stroke after another. Begun on a marked line it takes the marks off instead.
No Shift is needed, which Terminal of macOS and some others keep for themselves.
A click puts the cursor on a line and the marks stay, in a stroke of \fBM\fR the click goes on with it as the keys do;
\fBCtrl\fR+click marks a line or takes its mark off.
.TP
.B Ctrl-Ins
Copy the selected lines or the line under the cursor to the clipboard.
.TP
.B Ctrl-E
Export the selected lines or all of them to a file.
.TP
.B F8
Clear the window.
.TP
.B Ctrl-O\fR, \fBCtrl-S
Open, save the layout of all windows; both start at the layout opened or saved last, Save offers its file again.
.TP
.B Alt-0
List of the windows to go to, narrowed down by a filter; the window used before is under the cursor.
.TP
.B F12
Watches of plugins with their status: Enter shows the lines of a watch, Delete or Backspace removes it, U turns their
times to UTC and back; \fBWatch 1/3 *\fR in the
status line tells about new lines, a click on it opens the list.
.TP
.B Alt-X
Exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TURBOJEDI_CLIPBOARD_COPY
Command that takes copied lines, like \fB\-\-clipboard\-copy\fR.
.BR sudo (8)
leaves it out of the environment and runs the command as root; give it by the option then, going back to
your user, such as \fB\-\-clipboard\-copy "sudo \-u $USER $HOME/remote-copy.sh"\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/turbojedi/last.jedi
The layout kept on exit, \fI~/.config/turbojedi/last.jedi\fR without XDG_CONFIG_HOME.
It is opened on the next start unless a layout or filter options are given.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/turbojedi/screen.ini
Shadows of the windows chosen in Window, Shadows: \fBshadows=off\fR, \fBlight\fR, \fBmedium\fR or \fBdark\fR.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/turbojedi/allow.conf, /etc/turbojedi/allow.d/*.conf
Users answered on D-Bus besides the own one and root, a block each: \fBuser=\fR a name or a UID, or \fBgroup=\fR a
name or a GID for its members, then \fBmethods=\fR the methods it may call, all of them without it;
\fBInvoke:\fIplugin\fR or \fBInvoke:\fIplugin.method\fR for the methods of one plugin. See DBUS.md.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/turbojedi/plugins
Plugins of the user in Lua 5.4, \fI~/.config/turbojedi/plugins\fR without XDG_CONFIG_HOME.
.TP
.I /usr/share/turbojedi/plugins, /usr/local/share/turbojedi/plugins
Plugins of the package and of the machine; \fI/usr/share/turbojedi/examples\fR has more of them to link.
.TP
.I /usr/share/dbus-1/system.d/me.burnaway.TurboJedi.conf
The policy that lets TurboJedi take its name on the system bus, \fI/etc/dbus-1/system.d\fR for \fBmake install\fR.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/turbojedi-mcp.py
The server of the Model Context Protocol for AI agents.
.SH PLUGINS
The Actions menu of an entry is made by plugins in Lua 5.4: the \fI*.lua\fR files of the directories above, run
once at start in the order of their names. A file of the same name in the directory of the user takes the place
of the one of the package, an empty one leaves it out. Run by root, a plugin is used only when nobody but root,
or the group root or staff of /usr/local, can change it and every directory above it. Every action is a command
of a list of arguments, never a line of a shell, and changes nothing without being chosen. A plugin may also watch the
journal for the entries of a filter as they come while TurboJedi is open, with a status and lines of its own in the list
of F12; a layout keeps such watches for their plugins. PLUGINS.md of the package tells how to write them.
.SH D-BUS
Every instance takes the name \fBme.burnaway.TurboJedi.U\fIuid\fB.P\fIpid\fR on the system bus and answers its own
user and root, and the user of \fBsudo\fR of an instance of root. It reads the windows, the entries, the journal and
the watches, and changes only the screen: marks, the cursor, the moment of a window, a new window, shown in the status
line as \fBAPI: ...\fR until a key is pressed; such calls wait while a dialog is open. Without the policy above there
is no interface and nothing else changes. \fBturbojedi\-mcp.py\fR gives the interface to an AI agent over stdio,
\fBssh server turbojedi\-mcp.py\fR from the machine of the agent. DBUS.md and MCP.md of the package tell more.
.SH NOTES
Reading the whole journal needs root or the \fBsystemd-journal\fR group.
.SH AUTHOR
Artem Prilutskiy, https://github.com/cyanide-burnout/TurboJedi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR grc (1),
.BR sd-journal (3)
